The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says every report belongs to one parser bug. The
exported data supports two problem tickets. Five reports need more evidence, but they do not
establish another problem.

<Findings />

## Desk resolution on 5 October 2026

The [course update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) records both tickets as
closed:

- The parser ticket closed as fixed after `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- The hint ticket closed as won't fix because the `booking-v3` form was retired.

These resolutions are newer than the 4 October export. This repository did not deploy the
parser change or retire the form. The export remains the source for the historical report
counts below.

## What the current page shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The current table routes every report through `causeOf`. That function groups reports by the
tenant's locale, form version, and channel. It does not inspect the report body. The table
therefore places the temporal anomalies under `parser` and footnotes them there.

The table preserves the desk's exported classification of 379 parser and 33 hint reports.
Together, those two groups cover all 412 unique reports. The parser group includes five
reports that the desk footnotes for further investigation. The classification does not prove a
new cause for those five reports.

## The two problem tickets

### The shared parser reads day-first dates as month-first

The [parser ticket](https://github.com/RyanLisse/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) covers 379
reports across app, email, and form channels. The example reports support a day-first tenant
reaching the shared month-first parser. Five unusual reports remain counted here and footnoted
because the export does not establish another cause.

### Booking v3 gives a month-first hint to day-first tenants

The [hint ticket](https://github.com/RyanLisse/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) covers form
reports from day-first tenants that use `booking-v3`. The form tells
people to enter `MM/DD/YYYY`, then the product reads the date in the tenant's day-first
locale. The hint and the parser disagree.

## Five reports needing further investigation

- `R-41202` says the invite and calendar differed by one day.
- `R-41209` says June bookings were one day off.
- `R-41236` says the invite arrived before the booking existed.
- `R-41344` says recipients in Lisbon and Toronto received different weekdays.
- `R-41620` says Tuesday 6 October appeared as Monday.

The desk counts these reports where they are filed and footnotes them. Their bodies do not
confirm a separate cause. Runtime timestamps and delivery records are needed before the desk
can identify another problem.

## Why subject matching merged different problems

The accepted [subject-line decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) tells the desk to
merge reports when their normalized subjects match. The live explorer uses cause instead:

<DedupeExplorer />

Its five cases show the mismatch:

- App and email reports from `kettle.books` have different subjects but the same parser cause.
- Form and app reports from `alder.physio` share a subject but have different causes.
- Form reports from `copper.studio` and `morrow.tax` share a subject but have different causes.
- Form reports from `pale.harbour` and `alder.physio` have different subjects but the same hint cause.
- Reports for an unknown tenant have no supported cause, even when their subjects match.

Keep decision 0001 as history. Replace it with a new decision that merges only after the desk
has evidence that two reports share a cause. Footnote uncertain reports under the supported
cause. Create another ticket only when evidence establishes another problem.

## The queue kept moving

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The large merged bar in the week of 29 June records a desk batch on 2 July. It does not mark
the incident's start, a production change, or a recovery. Reports continued to arrive and the
desk continued to merge them through the export date.

